<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Pulmonary tuberculosis, smear-negative, Xpert-confirmed (A15.0)</content>. All three sputum smears are negative for acid-fast bacilli, but Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra on the first specimen detected Mycobacterium tuberculosis at a low level with no rifampin resistance, which with her symptoms and the right upper lobe opacities confirms pulmonary tuberculosis. Cultures and susceptibility testing are pending. HIV, hepatitis B surface antigen, hepatitis C antibody and the pregnancy test are negative. Liver and kidney tests are normal, hemoglobin A1c 5.1%, and visual acuity 85 letters in each eye with normal color vision. At 38.5 kg she is below the 40 kg needed for the four-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen, so the standard six-month regimen is used. Dr. Abernathy-Okoye agrees the regimen, doses and monitoring. [S]<list><item>Isoniazid 200 mg, rifampin 450 mg, pyrazinamide 1000 mg and ethambutol 600 mg daily, with pyridoxine 50 mg, from today; first dose taken in the clinic.</item><item>Daily video-observed therapy with the public health center from tomorrow.</item><item>Notification to the public health center today.</item><item>Liver tests and vision at two weeks; call the same day for nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, yellow eyes, dark urine or any change in vision.</item><item>Expect orange urine and tears.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Moderate protein-calorie malnutrition (E44.0)</content>. Weight 38.4 kg; eating slightly more. [S]<list><item>Second dietitian visit next week; supplement drinks twice daily.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Low income (Z59.6)</content>. Treatment costs are her main fear. Outpatient tuberculosis drugs, X-rays and sputum tests qualify for public funding of 95 percent under the Infectious Diseases Control Law. [S]<list><item>Medical social worker to complete the public funding application with her today.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Sputum smear and culture</td><td>At diagnosis, at two weeks, and at the end of the intensive phase (two months)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three specimens, one on waking: smears negative for acid-fast bacilli; liquid and solid cultures pending</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Xpert MTB/RIF Ultra (nucleic acid amplification with rifampin resistance)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Mycobacterium tuberculosis detected, low; rifampin resistance not detected</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Drug susceptibility testing of the first isolate</td><td>Once, on the first positive culture</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Phenotypic susceptibility to first-line drugs to be set up on the first positive culture</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>HIV test</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>HIV-1/2 antigen and antibody test negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Hepatitis B and C serology</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Hepatitis B surface antigen, surface antibody and core antibody negative (not immune); hepatitis C antibody negative</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Pregnancy test</td><td>Before starting treatment in a woman who could be pregnant</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Negative at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Diabetes screen (hemoglobin A1c)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Up to date</td><td>5.1% at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Liver tests (ALT, AST, bilirubin)</td><td>Baseline; at two weeks and monthly in the intensive phase because of low weight and albumin; then for symptoms</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal at intake (ALT 12, AST 19 U/L, bilirubin 0.4 mg/dL); albumin 3.3 g/dL</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Visual acuity and color vision</td><td>Baseline and monthly while on ethambutol</td><td>Up to date</td><td>85 letters in each eye with normal color vision at intake, before ethambutol</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Chest X-ray</td><td>At diagnosis and at the end of treatment as a new baseline</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Right upper lobe opacities without a cavity at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Regimen choice and weight-based doses</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Standard six-month regimen; not eligible for the four-month rifapentine-moxifloxacin regimen at 38.5 kg. Isoniazid 5, rifampin 12, pyrazinamide 26 and ethambutol 16 mg/kg</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Doses observed and missed (video-observed therapy)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>First dose observed in the clinic; video-observed therapy from tomorrow</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Rifampin drug-interaction review, including hormonal contraception</td><td>With every new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other medicines; told that rifampin weakens hormonal contraceptives and interacts with many drugs</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Home precautions and the criteria for release</td><td>Until two weeks of treatment with clinical improvement, for smear-negative disease</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Precautions at home continue; smear-negative, so treated as an outpatient</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Notification to the public health center (Infectious Diseases Control Law, Article 12)</td><td>At once on diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Notified today; the center was told of the suspected case at intake</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Contact investigation (household and close contacts)</td><td>Led by the public health center, from diagnosis</td><td>Referred</td><td>The public health center will assess her household and close contacts; their results belong to their own records</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Public funding and other help with costs</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Public funding application completed with the social worker</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Weight and nutrition</td><td>Every visit; dose review if weight changes by more than about 5 kg</td><td>Completed today</td><td>38.4 kg; dietitian and supplement drinks</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At diagnosis, at two months, and at the end of treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 8 at intake, item 9 scored 0</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Infectious diseases consultation</td><td>Once at diagnosis, then as advised</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today by Dr. Abernathy-Okoye</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Complete 182 daily doses within about six and a half months by video-observed therapy. In progress; target 1896-10-20 [S]</item><item>Sputum culture negative at two months. In progress; target 1896-10-20 [S]</item><item>Weight at least 44 kg (BMI about 20) by the end of treatment. In progress; target 1896-10-20 [S]</item><item>Keep writing on a paced schedule without losing income during treatment. In progress; target 1896-10-20 [S]</item><item>No drug-induced liver injury or loss of vision during treatment. In progress; target 1896-10-20 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Official American Thoracic Society/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention/Infectious Diseases Society of America Clinical Practice Guidelines: Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Tuberculosis (2016); Updates on the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible and Drug-Resistant Tuberculosis: An Official ATS/CDC/ERS/IDSA Clinical Practice Guideline (2025); WHO consolidated guidelines on tuberculosis: module 4: treatment: drug-susceptible tuberculosis treatment (2022); Official American Thoracic Society/Infectious Diseases Society of America/Centers for Disease Control and Prevention Clinical Practice Guidelines: Diagnosis of Tuberculosis in Adults and Children (2017); Interim Guidance: 4-Month Rifapentine-Moxifloxacin Regimen for the Treatment of Drug-Susceptible Pulmonary Tuberculosis - United States, 2022 (2022); An official ATS statement: hepatotoxicity of antituberculosis therapy (2006); Guidelines for the investigation of contacts of persons with infectious tuberculosis: recommendations from the National Tuberculosis Controllers Association and CDC (2005); 結核 (Tuberculosis): notification criteria under Article 12 of the Infectious Diseases Control Law (current); 結核医療費の公費負担 (Public funding of tuberculosis medical costs under Articles 37 and 37-2 of the Infectious Diseases Control Law) (current); Consensus statement: Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics and American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition: characteristics recommended for the identification and documentation of adult malnutrition (undernutrition) (2012)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Two-week review with liver tests, vision and a sputum specimen. Call the same day for hepatitis symptoms or any change in vision. Next scheduled visit 1896-05-01: two-week review on treatment: culture result, liver tests, vision, and release from precautions. [S]</paragraph></text>

